  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Drier and cooler today.\n\nThe cold front that brought widespread rain to the area yesterday \nhas now stalled across the Carolinas, and looks to remain over that \nregion through the weekend. Drier air is working its way into the \nlocal area, as dewpoints have been slowly falling into the low 60s \nto upper 50s in some locations. East to northeasterly flow is \nbringing in some moisture from the coastal Mid Atlantic, so it will \nstay partly to mostly cloudy for most of the day, until some more \nbreaks in the cloud cover can bring some sun. However, not enough \nsun to help temperatures significantly increase. Highs this \nafternoon will be in the 60s to low 70s, which is about 5 to 10 \ndegrees below normal in the mountains, and 10 to 20 degrees below \nnormal in the Piedmont. \n\nMost of the shower activity has shifted farther south into\ncentral NC, and so not confident in redevelopment by this\nafternoon, but cannot rule out a few stray showers later today \nover the mountains and along the VA/NC border.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Cooler than normal temperatures continue through\nthe weekend, with shower and storm chances returning to the \narea tomorrow into early next week.\n\nA surface high over the northern Mid Atlantic and New England will \nkeep northeast to easterly low level winds advecting some \ncooler air into the area, and so expecting these below normal \ntemperatures through the weekend. Temperatures will start to \nslowly increase after Sunday, as a more typical summertime \npattern resumes over the US, with broad upper troughing \npersisting in the east and a 500mb ridge building across the \nMountain West. Some moisture will move back into the area from \nvia return flow around the high and pooling along the front \nstalled across the southeastern US, while a couple shortwaves \ndrop into the area in the broad northwesterly flow aloft. This \nwill combine to bring shower and thunderstorm chances back into \nthe forecast tomorrow, and lingering through the weekend. At \nthis time, the probability for any severe thunderstorms is low, \nuntil early next week, when a stronger shortwave and surface \ncold front look to arrive from the Midwest. Confidence in timing\nis low, given differences among deterministic guidance, and the\nGEFS ML severe probability has low probability, between 15% and\n30%, Sunday through Tuesday, so difficult to pinpoint the day\nwith the highest severe potential at this time.",
